{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to quiet machines. Street sweepers murmur along empty avenues, traffic lights rehearse their colors for no audience, and bakery fans push warm air into the cold. A cyclist passes, carrying flowers, while windows slowly gather the pale reflection of morning above silent roofs and chimneys.\n\nI like this hour because nothing seems fixed yet. The day has plans, certainly, but they have not hardened into obligations. A missed train is still only a possibility. An unanswered message remains asleep. Even worry feels less convincing before coffee, as if anxiety also needs time to dress properly.\n\nSometimes I imagine collecting these early minutes in glass jars, labeling each one with its weather and season. February would hold blue shadows and the smell of wet wool. July would glow gold, crowded with birdsong. October would contain one bright leaf, spinning past a doorway before touching the ground.\n\nOf course, stored mornings would lose what makes them precious. Their beauty depends on vanishing. Light climbs the walls, engines start, doors open, and private intentions meet the stubborn world. Soon every hour acquires a purpose, measured by meetings, receipts, arrivals, and the small negotiations that keep ordinary life moving.\n\nStill, something of dawn survives beneath the noise. It appears when a stranger holds an elevator, when rain pauses during a walk, or when dinner tastes better than expected. These moments are tiny openings, reminders that time is not merely spent. Occasionally, it is noticed, welcomed, and quietly lived well.",
